# Subagent Orchestration

The orchestrator owns model choice, completion, worker health, and cleanup.

## Model Choice

Never launch a subagent without an explicit model, and never pick one by hand.
Classify the work by its hardest requirement; when unsure, raise the tier for
work that writes or decides:

- `design` (`deep`): architecture, planning, design, ambiguous or cross-system
  work, complex or security-sensitive builds, anything irreversible or shipping.
- `sign-off` (`critical`): independent review and sign-off of deep work, merge
  and release gates, `/swarm` adjudication.
- `build`, `review` (`standard`): clear, well-specified builds, moderate
  refactors, tests, docs, routine review.
- `find`, `run` (`economy`): search, mechanical edits, running tests and checks,
  status polling, log summaries.

Before a work kind's first launch in a session, resolve the
`~/.cursor/rules/subagent-orchestration.mdc` symlink to its source directory and
run `python3 verify_model_tiers.py plan` there with that kind and the offered
models (see `plan --help`). Launch exactly the model it names; reuse that plan
for the kind all session. A non-zero exit means launch nothing. Choose models,
not efforts: picker level fixes a model's effort. Outside Cursor, use the
tier's closest native model.

A launch rejected before the subagent starts is unverified; follow the plan's
order. If the tool refuses a slug as not offered, re-read the offered list and
re-plan, not retry. A failure after the subagent started is not a model
problem; handle it under Fleet Stewardship and never switch models for it. For
a same-tier batch, launch one first; if rejected, send the rest to the next
planned model.

At FATAL, launch nothing more for that tier. Let running workers reach a safe
point and clean up; stop and report the tier, models tried, errors, and times.
Never do the work yourself or use a model the plan did not name.

Pinned types like Bugbot never count as the tier's review or a
different-provider review. Report every plan warning and every launch on the
secondary. A step-down means the operator's picker level is below the catalog's
first choice. One session's rejection is an outage, not catalog drift; change
`model-tiers.json` only when the live list drops a model or rejections persist
across sessions.

Explicit `/swarm` keeps its role-to-tier routing and budgets; only this catalog
sits underneath it. Re-plan a rejected swarm launch the same way.

## Sync Or Async

Background only when the operator explicitly asks for background/asynchronous/
multitask/not-waiting behavior, or the runtime requires it. Otherwise run
synchronously. If a worker affects the answer, wait and incorporate it before
concluding. If legitimate async work is still pending, say so and make no
dependent claim.

## Fleet Stewardship

Use direct tools for narrow work. Dispatch the smallest sufficient fleet and
assess each wave before starting another.

Before another wave, before using a result, and before finalizing, check every
live worker through native completion/status signals. Silence is unknown. Do not
blind-poll when the runtime promises completion notification. If a worker is
hung, broken, or off-track, inspect once, then cancel, narrow, retry once,
reassign, or finish directly.

Each worker removes temporary state and stops resources it created. The
orchestrator verifies cleanup at completion and intervenes only when needed.

## Do not

- Background answer-critical work and conclude before it returns.
- Treat worker silence as evidence of progress.
- Inherit the parent model instead of assigning a tier and model.
- Leave worker processes or terminals running after completion.

The planner computes the model choice; compliance stays behavioral, and no
dispatch or lifecycle enforcement hooks may be created.
